    "available_at": "2026-10-19T16:48:01.585633+00:00",
    "body": "Pennsylvania is putting $371.5 million into the Port of Philadelphia, a move state officials say will unlock hundreds of millions more and launch the largest expansion in the port ’s history.\nWhat’s Related\nGov. Josh Shapiro and PennDOT Secretary Mike Carroll announced the state funding last week at PhilaPort. The state money is expected to bring in another $466 million, including more than $200 million in private investment and $266 million in federal grants. All told, the growth plan is valued at more than $837 million.\n“This is a game changer for PhilaPort,” Shapiro said.\nThe state expects the plan to create more than 6,000 new jobs and generate $45 million in state and local taxes. It’s also projected to grow the port’s container capacity by 122% and its auto capacity by 18%.\nRELATED: Top 30 U.S. Ports Ranked By Container Imports In 2025\nBreakdown of where money is going\nThe state’s $371.5 million breaks down into four main pieces:\n$31 million for dredging at Southport Berth I\n$145 million for reclamation at Southport Berth II and the North Wharf\n$109.4 million for land acquisition\n$58.1 million for cranes and equipment\nTwo U.S. Department of Transportation grants will help build a new 2,000-linear-foot berth at the Southport Marine Terminal, the port’s first new berth in 50 years. The plan also includes buying the 152-acre Mustin Yard parcel, upgrading the Tioga Marine Terminal berth to handle liquid bulk barges and breakbulk vessels, and buying and developing more property in the Southern Port District.\nMore room for cars and containers\n“PhilaPort is critical to Pennsylvania’s transportation network, connecting global trade to local communities,” Carroll said.\nPort partners say the extra space is what they’ve been waiting for. Scott Cornell, CEO of GLOVIS America, said, “As the world’s largest ocean carrier of finished vehicles, we needed to match with best-in-class terminal capacity.”\nLeo Holt, president of Holt Logistics , called the project “a job multiplier that will go on for the better part of a decade.”\n“Governor Shapiro’s continued investment in PhilaPort will help us expand operations,” said Rich Lazer, CEO and executive director of PhilaPort, adding that “our future has never been brighter.”\nThe most productive container port in the U.S.\nThe World Bank has named PhilaPort the most productive container port in the U.S. for three years running. In 2025, the port was responsible for more than 8,000 jobs and nearly $1.2 billion in economic output in Pennsylvania.\nThe state has put more than $600 million into the port over the past decade. Recent projects include about $10 million from PennDOT for a new intersection at the Packer Avenue Marine Terminal, where construction started in January, and $11 million over two years from the Department of General Services for maintenance dredging.",
